  "nameStory": "The Lesser Pine Katydid gets its name from being smaller than other katydids in its group and its preference for living near pine trees and other conifers. The 'lesser' part distinguishes it from larger meadow katydids, while 'pine' refers to its habitat association with evergreen forests.",
  "description": "The Lesser Pine Katydid is a slender, green grasshopper-like insect that's part of the singing insects family. These delicate creatures are smaller than most other meadow katydids, with long, thread-like antennae that can be twice as long as their body. Like other katydids, they're excellent jumpers and have wings that they use to create their distinctive chirping songs. Their green coloration helps them blend perfectly into the grasses and pine needles where they live.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Noticeably smaller than other meadow katydids, with a compact, delicate build that feels lightweight if gently handled",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Antennae",
      "description": "Extremely long, hair-thin antennae that constantly wave and twitch as the katydid explores its surroundings",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Coloration",
      "description": "Bright to pale green body that may have subtle brown markings, perfectly matching the color of fresh pine needles",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Body Shape",
      "description": "Slender, elongated build with a narrow head and delicate legs that feel fragile compared to bulkier grasshoppers",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Wings",
      "description": "Clear to slightly green-tinted wings that make a soft papery rustling sound when the insect moves",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Lesser Pine Katydids prefer areas where pine forests meet open meadows or grassy clearings, especially in sandy or well-drained soils. You'll find them in pine barrens, forest edges, and meadows surrounded by conifers throughout New England's lower elevations. They're particularly common in areas with white pine, pitch pine, and mixed pine-oak woodlands.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults are not yet active; eggs are overwintering in plant stems or soil near pine trees.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when adults emerge, feed, and begin their evening chorus of chirping songs that can be heard from pine forest edges.",
    "fall": "Adults continue singing and mating until the first hard frosts, with females laying eggs in plant stems before winter arrives.",
    "winter": "No visible activity; the species overwinters as eggs hidden in protected locations near their host plants."
  },
  "funFact": "Lesser Pine Katydids can 'hear' with their front legs! They have tiny ear-like structures called tympana on their front tibias that pick up the songs of other katydids, essentially giving them knees that function as ears.",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other meadow katydids in the same genus, but the Lesser Pine Katydid's smaller size and preference for pine-adjacent habitats help distinguish it. Memory trick: 'Lesser size, loves pines' - if you find a small katydid near evergreens, you've likely found your Lesser Pine Katydid.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called a 'pine' katydid, this species doesn't actually feed on pine trees but rather on grasses and small plants that grow in pine forest ecosystems. The name refers to its habitat preference rather than its diet, which can confuse beginning naturalists.",
  "compareHints": "Among its Orchelimum relatives, the Lesser Pine Katydid is distinguished by its smaller size and pine habitat association. While the Common Meadow Katydid prefers purely grassy areas and the Seaside Meadow Katydid sticks to coastal marshes, the Lesser Pine Katydid is the specialist of pine forest edges. It's also more delicately built than the robust Gladiator Meadow Katydid.",
